detail.abstract:
Maintaining water quality in the face of a changing climate is a challenge requiring cost effective water management policies. After several decades of command and control regulation, point sources of water pollution have decreased their effluent levels so that the main source of water pollution is now often nonpoint sources such as agricultural runoff. Water quality trading is an idea advocated by environmental economists as a cost effective way for point sources to avoid the cost of expensive effluent reductions by paying non-point sources to undertake certain best management practices.&nbsp;
<div>
 The first Canadian water quality trading program is underway in the South Nation watershed, southeast of Ottawa, Ontario. Compared to 37 other water quality trading programs in the United States, the South Nation Conservation (SNC) authority has created a significant number of offset projects, resulting in nontrivial pollutant reduction and the meeting of point source reduction requirements at a lower cost.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 However, the SNC nutrient trading program has some distinctive features that set it apart from other trading systems. Non-point sources have no requirements to reduce their runoff phosphorous content. Rather than buyers and sellers arranging their own transactions, the SNC purchases reductions by making grants to nonpoint sources and sells reductions to point sources for a set price per kilogram of required phosphorous reduction. This price is determined by the SNC by calculating the average cost of past projects plus administration and transaction costs. The revenue received by SNC is then used to subsidize the grants to non-point sources (farmers investing in best management practices).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 While SNC has been more successful in reducing pollution than other nutrient credit trading programs, after three years of operation this paper also finds that project offsets' cost per kilogram of phosphorous reduction often exceeds the price charged to point sources. A greater focus on offsets' cost effectiveness would result in greater pollution reduction within the watershed. Cost-effective nutrient credit trading programs are recommended for examination by other watershed organizations with significant pollution from non-point sources.
</div>
